-- title:
--   Minimal primes over varpi are the centres of two branch rings
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $K_0$, and let $F$ be a field that is an algebra over both $R$ and $K_0$ compatibly. Let $j \in F$ be nonzero and transcendental over $R$, and assume $F$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $K_0(j)$ generated by $j$ over $K_0$. Let $\varpi \in R$ generate the maximal ideal of $R$, write $A =$ `chartAlgFin R F j` for the subalgebra of elements of $F$ integral over the $R$-subalgebra generated by $j$, and let $W_0 \neq W_1$ be valuation subrings of $F$ such that, for each $i$ in the list $(W_0, W_1)$: the image of $R$ lies in $W_i$ and the image of the maximal ideal of $R$ lies in the nonunits of $W_i$; and for every $P \in R[X]$ whose reduction along the residue map of $R$ is nonzero, both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in $W_i$. Assume further that any valuation subring $V \subseteq F$ with these three properties equals $W_0$ or $W_1$. The conclusion is fourfold: (i) for every minimal prime $\mathfrak p$ over the ideal of $A$ generated by the image of $\varpi$, membership $b \in \mathfrak p$ is equivalent, for all $b \in A$, to $b$ lying in the nonunits of $W_0$, or else, for all $b \in A$, to $b$ lying in the nonunits of $W_1$; (ii) for each of $W_0$ and $W_1$ there is a minimal prime over that ideal whose members are exactly the elements of $A$ lying in the nonunits of that valuation subring; (iii) and (iv) some element of $A$ lies in the nonunits of $W_0$ but not of $W_1$, and some element of $A$ lies in the nonunits of $W_1$ but not of $W_0$.
--
--   The statement identifies the minimal primes of the special fibre of the $j$-finite chart of the two-chart integral model with the centres in $A$ of the two prescribed valuation subrings, and records that these two centres are incomparable, so the fibre has exactly two components. It is applied in the analysis of integral models of modular curves, where the two valuation subrings arise as the Gauss ring of the $q$-expansion and its Frobenius-twisted companion.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel_mem_minimalPrimes_span_iff_of_valuationSubring_pair.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
